WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.580
Most audiences agree that jump scares are
the bane of modern horror movies.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:08.840
Too many use them as a shortcut to frights,
sacrificing mood and atmosphere for a quick

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:11.080
bang that does nothing but startle.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:15.440
Like any tool in an artist's arsenal, however,
it all comes down to execution.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:19.940
A well-placed jump can serve as a satisfying
payoff to slow-burning tension, or it can

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:23.390
rocket you forward into a story with a burst
of thrilling adrenaline.

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:27.830
If you're curious about how an often-misused
trope can be successful, steel your nerves,

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:31.170
because we're about to dive headfirst into
some of the best scares that will make you

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:33.320
jump out of your seat.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:34.350
What's in the boat?

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:39.149
Jaws invented the summer blockbuster by deftly
blending charming humor, rollicking adventure,

00:00:39.149 --> 00:00:43.699
and insightful character moments, all punctuated
by a few well-placed scares.

00:00:43.699 --> 00:00:47.070
One of them in particular never fails to make
audiences scream.

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:50.989
Before the movie has even shown us the shark,
Hooper, played by Richard Dreyfuss, takes

00:00:50.989 --> 00:00:54.039
a scuba dive to investigate an abandoned fishing
boat.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:57.780
Just as he reaches out to check a tooth lodged
in the mangled hull, well…

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:10.480
After being unsatisfied with the scare in
test screenings, Spielberg re-shot the scene

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:13.470
in editor Verna Fields' swimming pool at his
own expense.

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:17.260
He was determined to get the timing just right,
and all that effort was worth it.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:21.330
Jaws still plays on big screens most summers,
and you can reliably watch the crowd ripple

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:24.480
with shock even four decades later.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:25.720
Raptor in the shed

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:30.250
Nearly 20 years after Jaws, Spielberg unleashed
another adventure blockbuster that combined

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:32.680
action with jump-out-of-your-skin scares:
Jurassic Park.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:36.241
While most of the park's dino denizens are
deadly in their own right, there's one in

00:01:36.241 --> 00:01:40.240
particular that has us terrified of maintenance
sheds all these years later.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.500
Throughout the movie, characters speak of
Velociraptors in hushed tones.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.750
Dr. Grant is clearly horrified to learn they're
being bred in the park, and game warden Robert

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:50.650
Muldoon warns everyone of the danger they
present.

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:54.671
But it isn't until Ellie Sattler, played by
Laura Dern, ventures into a maintenance shed

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:56.400
that we see just why the adult raptors are
so deadly.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:00.700
"Mr. Hammond, I think we're back in business!"

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:10.979
It's cool if you peed your pants a little,
it's kind of a rite of passage.

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:12.409
Closet corpse

00:02:12.409 --> 00:02:17.629
Gore Verbinski's remake of the Japanese movie
Ringu, 2002's The Ring is a quiet, eerie story

00:02:17.629 --> 00:02:20.379
with no gore or explicit violence in sight.

00:02:20.379 --> 00:02:24.220
It relies on uncanny imagery and unsettling
sounds to create an oppressive atmosphere

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:25.230
of dread.

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:28.549
This makes it all the more startling when
Verbinski does sneak in one big jump where

00:02:28.549 --> 00:02:30.140
you least expect it.

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:34.060
In a scene that otherwise lacks any serious
tension, Ruth Embry, portrayed by Lindsay

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:37.769
Frost, recounts the story of finding her daughter's
twisted corpse.

00:02:37.769 --> 00:02:39.580
Without warning, Verbinski cuts to ...

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:42.500
"I saw her face."

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:44.980
(When you finally watch R. Kelly's "Trapped in the Closet" in its entirety)

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:48.819
The movie never relies on jump scares afterward,
but the fear that this early shock might be

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:52.200
repeated serves to enhance the tension all
the way to the haunting final frame.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.200
"Don't you understand, Rachel?

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:00.260
She never sleeps."

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:03.060
The thing behind Winkie's

00:03:03.069 --> 00:03:07.560
While surrealist auteur David Lynch has long
been recognized as a master of the unsettling

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.750
and the uncanny, none of his films would comfortably
fit in the "horror" category.

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:15.459
That's what makes it extra startling when
he does go for moments of all-out terror,

00:03:15.459 --> 00:03:17.409
as he does in Mulholland Drive.

00:03:17.409 --> 00:03:22.109
Sitting in Winkie's Diner, Dan and Herb, played
by Patrick Fischler and Michael Cooke, respectively,

00:03:22.109 --> 00:03:25.390
discuss a nightmare the former had about a
man that he can see through the walls.

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:27.160
"I had a dream about this place."

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:29.000
"Oh boy."

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:29.780
"You see what I mean?"

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:32.180
"OK.

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:35.000
So you had a dream about this place."

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:39.420
What begins as a fairly lighthearted conversation
grows increasingly unnerving as Dan compares

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:40.919
the dream to reality.

00:03:40.919 --> 00:03:44.930
The two men walk to the back of the diner,
and, in typical Lynchian fashion, some truly

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:46.700
creepy stuff ensues.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.440
It's a simple enough moment, but highly effective
in capturing the surreal-yet-mundane logic

00:03:59.459 --> 00:04:01.180
of a nightmare.

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:02.669
Death from above

00:04:02.669 --> 00:04:06.549
Alfred Hitchcock's suspense masterpiece Psycho
laid the foundation for the entire slasher

00:04:06.549 --> 00:04:11.470
film genre, but it's mainly remembered for
one particular sequence: the iconic shower

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:12.470
scene.

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:16.799
However, one Psycho scene remains a startling
surprise for newcomers and fans alike.

00:04:16.799 --> 00:04:20.840
When private investigator Milton Arbogast,
portrayed by Martin Balsam, enters the Bates

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:25.490
house looking for clues, Hitchcock begins
establishing a tension that's vaguely unsettling,

00:04:25.490 --> 00:04:29.550
catching Arbogast glancing at each door and
hallway before he begins slowly and carefully

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:31.199
climbing the stairs.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:32.380
Suddenly …

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:43.660
Arbogast's stunned tumble down the stairs
was achieved by standing Balsam in front of

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:45.470
a rear-projection falling shot.

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:49.670
The result feels just slightly wrong, enhancing
the feeling that we, too, have just been the

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:51.520
victim of a sneak attack.

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:53.349
The Phantom unmasked

00:04:53.349 --> 00:04:57.639
It may be tempting to cite jump scares as
a symptom of Hollywood running out of ideas,

00:04:57.639 --> 00:05:01.990
but they're almost as old as movies themselves,
and the good ones still work.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:06.210
One great example of this trick comes from
Universal's 1925 silent production of The

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:07.599
Phantom of the Opera.

00:05:07.599 --> 00:05:12.009
Lon Chaney was the first major icon of horror
films, rising to prominence during the silent

00:05:12.009 --> 00:05:14.169
era as "the Man of a Thousand Faces."

00:05:14.169 --> 00:05:18.240
He crafted his own make-up effects, and there
was seemingly no end to the amount of discomfort

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.090
he was willing to put himself through to spook
his fans.

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:25.370
For Phantom, he devised an elaborate construction
of wires and paint that gave him an alarmingly

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:27.009
skull-like face.

00:05:27.009 --> 00:05:31.120
Chaney's creepy mug is first revealed when
Christine, played by Mary Philbin, slowly

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:33.940
works up the courage to pull off his mask.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:37.349
Stories of viewers fainting in the theater
at the film's premiere quickly became something

00:05:37.349 --> 00:05:38.900
of a Hollywood legend.

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:43.050
How much Universal may have played up these
reports in advertising remains difficult to

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:47.440
say, but it's undeniable that the shock remains
effective, even 90 years later.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:48.560
Thanks for watching!

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:50.960
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:53.090
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

